<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kunjali Marakkar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">16th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kunjali Marakkar was the title of the hereditary Muslim naval commanders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in the 16th century. Four successive Kunjalis led fleets of fast, light vessels against the Portuguese along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a>. The last, Kunjali IV, fell out with the Zamorin and was captured in a joint Portuguese and Calicut attack on his fort, then executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a> in 1600.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kunjali-marakkar.jpg" alt="Kunjali Marakkar" width="320" height="380" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krishna Chaitanya Velaga · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Kunhali_Marakkar_III_at_Visakha_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Marakkars came from a branch of Arab merchants of the seafaring community who had settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Kochi</a> and at first traded and worked with the Portuguese. According to Zainuddin Makhdoom II, author of the Tuhfat-ul-Mujahideen, they turned against them by 1524, when the Portuguese began buying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and other goods directly from the local people of Kochi and so broke into the trade of the Marakkars. The four Kunjali Marakkars are credited with organising the first naval defence of the Indian coast.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kutty Ahmed Ali, the first Kunjali, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a>'s navy when it helped the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceylon">Ceylon</a> against the Portuguese in 1524 and reduced the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombo">Colombo</a>. In 1525, after the Portuguese built a fortress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>, his ships bombarded it, and later that year he entered the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kochi">Cochin</a>, set fire to a number of Portuguese vessels and returned safely. In 1529 the Zamorin's navy defeated the Portuguese at the battle of the Chetwai river.</p><p>Their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> were built in four harbours that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> granted to the family, Muttungal, Vadakara, Chambal and Kaniyaram Kottu, each fortified on its sea side; repeated Portuguese attacks on them, above all on Vadakara, failed or cost the attackers dear.</p><p>The last Kunjali broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> by cutting off the tail of the ruler's elephant and assaulting a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nair</a> noble and his wife sent to demand an explanation. A first joint attack on his fort failed for lack of coordination; in the second the Portuguese struck from the sea and the Zamorin from the land. He surrendered to the Zamorin on a promise of pardon and was handed over to the Portuguese with his Chinese lieutenant Chinali, a boy he had rescued who was said to have been enslaved on a Portuguese ship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Portuguese historian Diogo do Couto, who questioned the captives, accused Chinali of torturing Christian prisoners, a claim no other source supports. At Iringal, north of Kozhikode, a hut that belonged to the family now holds a small museum of swords, cannonballs and knives, kept by the state's archaeology <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, and at Madhavan Kurichi in Thoothukudi district villagers worship Kunjali Maraikkayar as a deity. The Indian Navy commissioned INS Kunjali in their memory on 11 July 1954.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kunjali Marakkar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
